# =============================================================================
# Used Car Marketplace - backend environment template
# =============================================================================
#
# Copy this file, then edit the copied values. Nothing here is a live value:
#
#   cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
#
# Both of the repository's own instructions already expect exactly that:
#   scripts/setup_environment.sh:L40  ->  cp .env.example .env
#   README.md:L48-L49                 ->  "Copy `.env.example` to `.env`" /
#                                         "Update the variables in `.env` with
#                                          your specific configuration"
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# backend/app/core/config.py:L25 evaluates `settings = Settings()` AT IMPORT
# TIME, and the first eight fields of that class (L5-L12) are declared with NO
# default. Importing ANY module in the `app.*` tree therefore raises a
# pydantic.ValidationError naming all eight unless every one of them is
# resolvable. This template is what makes them discoverable: the block below is
# the complete, minimum set required to import the application at all.
#
# HOW THESE VALUES ARE READ - AND WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT DO
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# backend/app/core/config.py:L21-L23 sets `env_file = ".env"` with
# `env_file_encoding = "utf-8"`, so plain `KEY=value` lines in backend/.env
# populate the `Settings` object with no `export` prefix and no shell sourcing.
# Values already present in the real process environment take precedence over
# the file, which is how CI and container deployments override it.
#
# THE LIMIT OF THAT MECHANISM, which matters for the last section of this file:
# pydantic v1 reads the file only to build `Settings`, and only for the fields
# `Settings` DECLARES. It does NOT export anything into `os.environ`. So a key
# written here is invisible to any consumer that reads the process environment
# directly - which is exactly how the Google client libraries pick up
# FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST, STORAGE_EMULATOR_HOST and
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS. Those three are documented at the bottom of
# this file as REAL ENVIRONMENT EXPORTS for that reason; putting them in
# backend/.env alone has no effect whatsoever.
#
# One consequence of the shell-export path: this file is written to be safe to
# source. Every value that a shell would otherwise mangle - a name containing
# spaces, a JSON array containing double quotes - is quoted, so both of these
# work and agree with each other:
#
#   pydantic:  Settings() reads backend/.env directly (quotes are stripped)
#   shell:     cd backend && set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#
# Reading the file half of the pydantic path requires python-dotenv, which
# pydantic v1 uses for `env_file` support. It needs no requirement line of its
# own: `uvicorn[standard]==0.23.2` in backend/requirements.txt declares
# `python-dotenv>=0.13` in its `standard` extra, so installing the manifest
# supplies it. Without it pydantic raises `ImportError: python-dotenv is not
# installed` as soon as a .env file exists, so install the manifest first:
#   pip install -r backend/requirements.txt
#
# CONVENTIONS USED BELOW
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * Keys appear in the SAME ORDER they are declared in Settings, so this file
#     can be diffed against backend/app/core/config.py line by line.
#   * Each key carries a one-line note naming the code that reads it.
#   * Values are unquoted except where quoting is genuinely required to keep
#     the file safe to source in a shell - a value containing whitespace or
#     double quotes. python-dotenv strips a matching pair of surrounding
#     quotes, so the quoted and sourced forms produce the same value.
#   * EVERY VALUE HERE IS AN ILLUSTRATIVE PLACEHOLDER, NOT A CREDENTIAL. This
#     file is committed to source control. Real secrets belong in backend/.env
#     (already git-ignored) or in your platform's secret manager - never here.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED - no defaults. The application cannot be imported without all eight.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Human-readable application name. backend/app/core/config.py:L5. Quoted
# because the value contains spaces: unquoted, a shell sourcing this file would
# assign only `Used` and then try to run `Car` as a command. python-dotenv
# strips the quotes, so `Settings().PROJECT_NAME` is the same either way.
PROJECT_NAME="Used Car Marketplace"

# Root path prefix for the versioned API. Read by
# backend/app/core/security.py:L10 to build the OAuth2 `tokenUrl`
# (f'{settings.API_V1_STR}/auth/login'), so it must match the prefixes that
# backend/app/main.py mounts the routers under.
API_V1_STR=/api

# Symmetric JWT signing key - backend/app/api/auth.py:L37 (encode) and L49
# (decode), plus backend/app/core/security.py:L25,L33. REPLACE THIS BEFORE ANY
# DEPLOYMENT: the value below is illustrative and is NOT a usable secret. Every
# issued token is forgeable by anyone who knows the key. Generate a real one:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
SECRET_KEY=replace-with-a-long-random-secret-at-least-32-chars

# Access-token lifetime in minutes. Integer, no units.
# RECORDED FINDING (pre-existing, not fixed by this template): no code reads
# this setting today. backend/app/api/auth.py:L30-L38 hardcodes
# `timedelta(minutes=15)` at L35, and backend/app/core/security.py:L23 repeats
# the same hardcode, so both ignore the value configured here. The setting is
# still REQUIRED by Settings, so it must be present for the app to import.
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30

# Google Cloud project id. Read AT IMPORT TIME by
# backend/app/db/firestore.py:L5, which constructs
# `Client(project=settings.GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT)`, and also by
# backend/app/db/cloud_storage.py:L4 and
# backend/app/services/document_processing.py:L25,L49.
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=my-gcp-project-id

# Cloud Storage bucket holding listing photos and maintenance documents. Read
# at import time by backend/app/db/cloud_storage.py:L5. Bucket name only - no
# `gs://` scheme and no trailing slash.
GOOGLE_CLOUD_STORAGE_BUCKET=my-gcp-bucket-name

# Stripe secret key. Read at import time by
# backend/app/services/payment.py:L12 (`stripe.api_key = settings.STRIPE_API_KEY`
# - the module-level credential seat that stripe 7.9.0 exposes).
# Use a TEST-MODE key for development - the test-mode prefix is the one shown
# below. A LIVE-MODE key must never be written into this file, which is
# committed, nor pasted into backend/.env; keep live credentials in a secret
# manager. Naming the live prefix literally is avoided here on purpose, so this
# template cannot trip an automated credential scanner.
STRIPE_API_KEY=sk_test_replace_with_your_stripe_test_key

# Stripe webhook signing secret, used to verify that inbound webhook payloads
# genuinely came from Stripe. Copy it from the Stripe dashboard's webhook
# endpoint configuration; it always starts with `whsec_`.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_replace_with_your_stripe_webhook_secret


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL - already declared with a default in Settings.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Sentry error-reporting endpoint - backend/app/core/config.py:L13 declares it
# as `Optional[str] = None`. Deliberately left COMMENTED rather than set empty:
# an active `SENTRY_DSN=` line would make the setting the empty string instead
# of None, which reads as "configured but blank" to any `is not None` check.
# Uncomment and paste your project's DSN to enable reporting. Shape:
# SENTRY_DSN=https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PEER RATING AND REVIEW FEATURE (F-010) - all optional
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EVERY key in this section is declared WITH A DEFAULT in Settings
# (backend/app/core/config.py:L14-L19), so omitting any of them cannot break
# application startup. That is a correctness property rather than a
# convenience: because `settings = Settings()` is evaluated at import time, a
# new *required* field would make the application unimportable in every
# environment that had not yet been updated. The values shown below ARE the
# in-code defaults, restated here so the tunables are discoverable.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# JWT signing/verification algorithm. Referenced by BOTH auth modules -
# backend/app/api/auth.py:L37,L49 and backend/app/core/security.py:L25,L33 -
# and previously declared in neither. HS256 is correct because both call sites
# sign with the symmetric SECRET_KEY above; an asymmetric algorithm (RS256 and
# friends) would need a key pair instead.
ALGORITHM=HS256

# Inclusive lower bound of the rating scale. Enforced server-side by the
# Pydantic bounds in backend/app/schema/rating.py and mirrored client-side by
# the Zod schema in frontend/src/schema/rating.ts.
RATING_MIN=1

# Inclusive upper bound of the rating scale. 1..5 is fixed by the project's own
# success KPI - "4.5/5 star average rating from both buyers and sellers" - so
# changing RATING_MIN or RATING_MAX here without changing the Pydantic and Zod
# bounds too would make client and server disagree about a valid rating.
RATING_MAX=5

# Maximum length, in characters, of the optional free-text review body. Bounded
# server-side by backend/app/schema/rating.py; the submission form mirrors the
# same limit so the counter and the server agree.
RATING_REVIEW_MAX_LENGTH=2000

# Double-blind reveal window, in days. A submitted rating stays unpublished -
# and is excluded from the recipient's aggregate - until the counterparty
# submits theirs or this many days elapse since submission, whichever comes
# first. This is what removes the incentive for retaliatory rating.
RATING_WINDOW_DAYS=14

# Browser origins allowed to call the API with credentials. Read AT IMPORT TIME
# by backend/app/main.py:L28 (`allow_origins=settings.ALLOWED_ORIGINS`).
# Pydantic v1 parses a List[str] field from a JSON array, hence the bracketed
# form; keep it valid JSON with double-quoted members. http://localhost:3000 is
# the frontend development origin (README.md:L56).
#
# Wrapped in SINGLE quotes so the file stays safe to source: the value's own
# double quotes are part of the JSON and must survive verbatim. python-dotenv
# strips the single-quote pair, and a shell passes the inner text through
# unchanged, so pydantic receives the same `["http://localhost:3000"]` on both
# paths. Unquoted, a shell would swallow the double quotes and hand pydantic
# `[http://localhost:3000]`, which is not valid JSON and fails validation.
#
# SECURITY: narrow this to your real origins before any deployment. Never use
# ["*"] here - backend/app/main.py:L29-L31 pairs this list with
# `allow_credentials=True` and wildcard methods and headers, so a wildcard
# origin would let any site issue credentialed cross-origin requests. Tightening
# this list is the correct lever; the CORS middleware block itself is out of
# scope for this change and must not be edited.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS='["http://localhost:3000"]'


# =============================================================================
# RECORDED FINDINGS - referenced by code but NOT declared in Settings
# =============================================================================
# The four names in this section are COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE and are
# documentation of a gap, NOT a deliverable of this change. Declaring these
# fields is outside the scope of the rating feature.
#
# All four are reached by attribute lookup on the `settings` object, and
# pydantic v1 exposes ONLY declared fields - so uncommenting a line here cannot
# make the attribute exist. Each access still raises AttributeError until
# backend/app/core/config.py declares the field. They are recorded with working
# example values so the fix is a one-line declaration when it is scheduled.
#
# A DIFFERENT kind of undeclared name follows in the next section: those are
# read from the process environment rather than from `Settings`, so for them the
# question is not whether a field exists but whether the value reached
# os.environ. Do not treat the two sections the same way.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Celery broker URL - backend/app/tasks/background_jobs.py:L9
# (`Celery('used_car_marketplace', broker=settings.CELERY_BROKER_URL)`).
# The worker tier is aspirational: no task in the codebase is ever dispatched
# and no broker is provisioned, which is exactly why the rating feature's
# correctness does NOT depend on it - the read endpoints publish an expired
# rating opportunistically when they encounter one.
# CELERY_BROKER_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# Document AI / Vision region - backend/app/services/document_processing.py:L25
# and L49 interpolate it into
# f"projects/{GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT}/locations/{GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION}".
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION=us-central1

# Document AI processor id used to extract maintenance-record fields -
# backend/app/services/document_processing.py:L27. Copy the processor id (a
# short hexadecimal string) from the Document AI console.
# DOCUMENT_AI_PROCESSOR_ID=

# Document AI processor id used to classify uploaded documents -
# backend/app/services/document_processing.py:L51. A second processor id from
# the same console, distinct from the extractor above.
# DOCUMENT_AI_CLASSIFIER_ID=

# =============================================================================
# PROCESS-ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES - these must be REAL ENVIRONMENT EXPORTS
# =============================================================================
# The three names below are read by the Google client libraries STRAIGHT FROM
# THE PROCESS ENVIRONMENT (`os.environ`), not from `Settings`. They are not
# Settings fields, and - the part that is easy to get wrong - pydantic's
# `env_file` support does NOT put them there: it parses backend/.env only to
# populate declared `Settings` fields and exports nothing. So writing these
# three into backend/.env and expecting them to take effect DOES NOT WORK.
#
# They still matter here, because backend/app/db/firestore.py:L5,
# backend/app/db/cloud_storage.py:L4-L5, backend/app/services/ai_vision.py and
# backend/app/services/document_processing.py each construct their Google
# client at MODULE IMPORT TIME - so credentials, or an emulator standing in for
# them, must already be in the environment before those modules can be imported
# at all. They are documented in this file so the set of things a developer has
# to arrange is discoverable in one place.
#
# Set them one of these three ways - all three put the value in os.environ,
# which is the only thing that works:
#
#   1. Export them in the shell that starts the process:
#        export FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=localhost:8080
#        export GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/abs/path/to/key.json
#        python -m uvicorn app.main:app --reload
#
#   2. Keep them in this file's copy and let the SHELL export them, which is
#      what `set -a` is for. Because every value in this template is quoted
#      where a shell needs it to be, sourcing is safe:
#        cd backend && set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#      Note what this buys you: the shell exports EVERY key, so `Settings`
#      still sees the ones it declares (from the environment rather than from
#      the file) and the Google libraries additionally see these three.
#
#   3. Declare them as container/platform environment variables - `environment:`
#      in a compose service, `env`/`envFrom` on a Kubernetes pod spec, or the
#      deployment platform's own configuration. This is the right answer for
#      anything beyond local development.
#
# The three entries are left COMMENTED because none of them is wanted in a
# real-project deployment: each either redirects a client at an emulator or
# points at a key file whose path is machine-specific. Uncomment the ones your
# local setup needs AND use path 2 or 3 above, or export them directly.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Point the Firestore client at a local emulator instead of a real project. No
# credentials are needed while this is set. Start the emulator with:
#   gcloud emulators firestore start --host-port=0.0.0.0:8080
# FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=localhost:8080

# Make google-cloud-storage build an anonymous client instead of demanding real
# credentials, which is enough to import the module. There is no local GCS
# emulator in this project, so an actual upload or download still needs a real
# bucket and real credentials.
# STORAGE_EMULATOR_HOST=http://localhost:9199

# Absolute path to a service-account key file. Vision AI and Document AI have
# no emulator, so their client constructors need a loadable key even offline.
# Keep the key OUTSIDE the repository: the root .gitignore excludes
# `service-account*.json` and `*.pem` precisely so a key is never committed.
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/service-account-key.json
